## 4. Acceptance criteria → test cases (traceability core)

Every acceptance criterion from the PRD maps to ≥1 test case. **`Mode`** tags who owns
each case's automation:
- **`🔵 dev`** — covered by a **developer** test (unit / integration / contract). QA
  references it for traceability but does **not** author it. **All API-level cases
  (contract, negative, boundary, ETag, authz allow/deny) are `🔵 dev`** — QA never
  writes api/integration tests (`testing.md`, `api-contracts.md`, ADR-053).
- **`🤖 qa`** — a QA-authored **`test/qa/` browser** spec (a11y / console / visual /
  journey / affordance-visibility). The **only** thing `/write-qa-cases` generates.
- **`✋ manual`** — QA manual / human judgment.

`Spec` links the test id or `manual`. `Result` rolls up into the release report.

| AC | Test case | Mode | Type | Spec / id | Last result |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| AC-1 | <API / contract / ETag / authz case> | 🔵 dev | integration | `<feature>-lifecycle.integration.spec.ts` | — |
| AC-1 | <visual / a11y / affordance case> | 🤖 qa | visual · a11y | `test/qa/…` | ⬜ not run |
| AC-2 | <exploratory / UX-judgment case> | ✋ manual | manual | manual | ⬜ |

Mode legend: `🔵 dev` = developer test (not QA-authored) · `🤖 qa` = QA `test/qa/` browser spec (input to `/write-qa-automation`) · `✋ manual` = QA manual.
Result legend: ✅ pass · ❌ fail (→ `/bug`) · ⬜ not run · ⏭️ skipped (reason).

